{
  "case_id": "2010-10-98",
  "document_hash": "6f7a65bb1b5dadb489929153fba78e3565a7981ccb91316a1a62e53e9a654b90",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2010-10-98",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "6f7a65bb1b5dadb489929153fba78e3565a7981ccb91316a1a62e53e9a654b90",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6f7a65bb1b5d-0000-0002",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:18 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "General Katyal",
      "case_id": "2010-10-98",
      "confidence": 0.93,
      "document_hash": "6f7a65bb1b5dadb489929153fba78e3565a7981ccb91316a1a62e53e9a654b90",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6f7a65bb1b5d-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "There are three reasons why the Petitioner should not be personally liable for money damages. The first is because the prosecutor's act of seeking the material witness warrant is integrally associated with the judicial process and entitled to absolute immunity."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2010-10-98",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6f7a65bb1b5dadb489929153fba78e3565a7981ccb91316a1a62e53e9a654b90",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6f7a65bb1b5d-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Do you acknowledge that it\nis then constitutional? Your -- your opening comments\nmake me think you don't even acknowledge that it's\nconstitutional then?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Lee Gelernt",
      "case_id": "2010-10-98",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6f7a65bb1b5dadb489929153fba78e3565a7981ccb91316a1a62e53e9a654b90",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6f7a65bb1b5d-0001-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I would say that based on the\nlegal historian's brief there is a strong argument to be made that it is not constitutional, with respect at\nleast to cooperative witnesses."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Lee Gelernt",
      "case_id": "2010-10-98",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6f7a65bb1b5dadb489929153fba78e3565a7981ccb91316a1a62e53e9a654b90",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6f7a65bb1b5d-0001-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Our argument is that it cannot be used for\nulterior purposes."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Lee Gelernt",
      "case_id": "2010-10-98",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6f7a65bb1b5dadb489929153fba78e3565a7981ccb91316a1a62e53e9a654b90",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6f7a65bb1b5d-0001-0007",
      "kind": "allegation",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the sole reason this arrest was made\nwas not to secure testimony but to preventively detain\nand investigate someone for whom there was no probable\ncause or violation of the law."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2010-10-98",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6f7a65bb1b5dadb489929153fba78e3565a7981ccb91316a1a62e53e9a654b90",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6f7a65bb1b5d-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Is this a -- is this a\nrealistic hypothetical that you've posed? Now, in order\nto detain someone under the material witness statute,\nthat person, potential witness, must have material\ntestimony, not just relevant testimony, material\ntestimony, testimony that would be of some importance in\nthe criminal prosecution."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2010-10-98",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6f7a65bb1b5dadb489929153fba78e3565a7981ccb91316a1a62e53e9a654b90",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6f7a65bb1b5d-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: Just a point of detail.\nI -- I may not be recalling correctly. You said this\nstatute is rarely used. I thought there were 4,000\nmaterial witness hearings a year. Is that mostly\nbecause of the immigration?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2010-10-98",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6f7a65bb1b5dadb489929153fba78e3565a7981ccb91316a1a62e53e9a654b90",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6f7a65bb1b5d-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 29,
        "start": 29
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Your argument is that the\nConstitution does not allow a material witness to be\ndetained, so long as the witness says in court that he\nor she will show up for trial, no matter how much\nevidence there is that this person poses a great risk of\nflight? If the person says in court, I will be there,\nthat's the end of it, the person cannot be detained?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2010-10-98",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6f7a65bb1b5dadb489929153fba78e3565a7981ccb91316a1a62e53e9a654b90",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6f7a65bb1b5d-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 37,
        "start": 37
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: You seem to acknowledge that in -- a dual motive case would not violate the Fourth Amendment, or wouldn't necessarily violate the Fourth Amendment; isn't that right?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Gelernt",
      "case_id": "2010-10-98",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6f7a65bb1b5dadb489929153fba78e3565a7981ccb91316a1a62e53e9a654b90",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6f7a65bb1b5d-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. GELERNT: I think it actually is, Your Honor, and the reason is because I think it's -- I think it actually gives cushion to the reasonable official, because I think once you are saying we want to secure testimony, it might be very difficult, as the Chief Justice was pointing out, to say, well, how I do know if I could have ulterior motives or not? That might be a very difficult situation."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2010-10-98",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6f7a65bb1b5dadb489929153fba78e3565a7981ccb91316a1a62e53e9a654b90",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6f7a65bb1b5d-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Where did you allege that the desire to detain was the sole motive for this?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Gelernt",
      "case_id": "2010-10-98",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6f7a65bb1b5dadb489929153fba78e3565a7981ccb91316a1a62e53e9a654b90",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6f7a65bb1b5d-0002-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. GELERNT: I think, you know, part of what the Court's answer is, it's a practical concern. That it's just too difficult to have different immunities, and the Court -- so the Court has repeatedly reaffirmed that, and I think from a policy standpoint, a practical standpoint, it's felt that that's the right analysis, and there has to be some way to tether the immunity analysis; and history is ultimately, I think -\u00ad what the Court has said it's a necessary though not sufficient, and that once you sort of unmoor it from history, it becomes very difficult to keep the two."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court Transcript",
      "case_id": "2010-10-98",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6f7a65bb1b5dadb489929153fba78e3565a7981ccb91316a1a62e53e9a654b90",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6f7a65bb1b5d-0002-0008",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 53
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "consistent with their newly minted standard, a dual motive standard."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2010-10-98",
      "confidence": 0.0,
      "document_hash": "6f7a65bb1b5dadb489929153fba78e3565a7981ccb91316a1a62e53e9a654b90",
